  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 1109 PM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nExtended range guidance from the lastest suite of global models \ncontinues to indicate that our forecast area will remain under the\ninfluence of moderately strong NNW flow aloft and dry northerly \nflow at the surface from Wednesday night-Friday. With dewpoints \nfalling into the 60s, overnight lows will respond by dropping to \nmore comfortable levels (m-u 60s), with afternoon highs remaining \nin the u80s-l90s. However, it appears as if deep-layer moisture \nwill quickly return to the region at some point Friday \nnight/Saturday morning as a compact surface low shifts eastward \nfrom the southern Plains into the Lower-MS Valley. Although strong\nlow-level warm/moist advection ahead of the low will contribute \nto an increase in the coverage of convection across our region, \ncurrent thinking is that instability will remain low (at least \ncompared to the past couple of weeks) and should limit the overall\ncoverage of thunderstorms. That said, the low-level jet will \nlikely increase considerably ahead of this system, and may yield \nat least a limited risk for storm organization on Friday \nnight/Saturday. By Sunday, the surface low is expected to weaken \nand transition into a broad trough across the Gulf Coast states, \nwhich should lead to a gradual decrease in the coverage of \nprecipitation across our region.",
